   ],
   "headline": "NIST names the gaps in incident response for AI agents",
   "summary": "NIST IR 8621 reports on a workshop of more than 400 experts. It finds that traditional monitoring can miss prompt injection, shifts in model behavior and unauthorized agent actions. Priorities include reasoning-layer telemetry, governance of non-human identities, and containment scoped to what an agent is allowed to do.",
   "curriculum_note": "Add an agent incident drill: students trace a prompt-injection incident through tool logs, then revoke the agent's credentials without taking down the whole app.",
   "details": [
    "Planned follow-ups include an update to SP 800-61 for AI-related incidents and a NIST AI Consortium task group on agent monitoring and containment.",
    "For procurement, NIST notes vendors often hold key records, so contracts should cover audit rights, incident notification and evidence."
   ],
